{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>באדין</b> — Then Daniel replied with counsel and reason, and behold it is like כמו שבעה יעטוהי. Its meaning: like one of the seven who answer reason and counsel, Potiphar, chief officer of Pharaoh. In my opinion it is not like רב טבחיא, for the word טבח in the sacred tongue is interpreted in two ways: the first, slaughter, like רב טבחים, and the proof is די נפק לקטלא, and like it כצאן לטבח יובל, and many like these. The second meaning, like in the Ishmaelite tongue, cook, like וטבוח טבח והכן, for there was no person at that time who slaughtered sheep in Egypt for stews and cooked dishes, for the women do not slaughter, and also וירם הטבח. And because the chief of the bakers was the one baking bread and the chief of the cupbearers his colleague, therefore I said that he placed them in the house of colleagues, which is the chief of the cooks, and did not place them in the house of the prison where all the prisoners are confined, because of the greatness of their rank, that they were officers."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes several Aramaic/Hebrew phrases from Daniel and Genesis; the cryptic phrase 'like one of the seven' he leaves unexplained. The reference to 'the Ishmaelite tongue' (Arabic) for טבח as 'cook' is his standard comparative method."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:19:16.252901463Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}